{
  "schema": "ci-budget-calibration/1",
  "repository": "seunghyukchoe/copper-mcp",
  "recorded": "2026-08-14",
  "why": "The v0.7.0 release run was cancelled by its own 20-minute budget and the release shipped without provenance (D-196). The budget was never wrong about the machine it was measured on -- it was measured on the wrong machine. A local `make check` at 20m29s says nothing about a hosted ubuntu-latest runner, where the same gate takes 34m59s. This file is the measurement the budget is derived from, so that `scripts/check_ci_budgets.py` compares a declared ceiling against a recorded duration rather than against a memory.",
  "source": "GitHub Actions REST API, read-only: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}/jobs. `seconds` is completed_at - started_at for the hosted job, which includes runner setup, checkout and the post-steps -- the same wall clock `timeout-minutes` bounds.",
  "half_rule": "A budget must be at least twice the longest recorded duration of the job it bounds. Stated the other way, which is how the release workflow's own comment states it: when the measured gate exceeds half of a CI timeout budget, raise the budget in the same pull request that grew the gate. A timeout is a calibrated ceiling like max_fill_vertices, not a constant.",
  "update": "Re-record before raising or lowering any budget, and whenever a change is expected to move a job's duration: read the durations from the API for the newest completed runs of the affected job, replace that job's `observations` list, and re-run `python scripts/check_ci_budgets.py`. Append rather than replace only when the older runs are still evidence about the current tree. Every recorded observation binds, not just the newest one, so a stale slow run keeps the budget honest rather than being averaged away. See docs/development.md, `CI budgets are calibrated, not chosen`.",
  "conclusion": "Every observation records the run's `conclusion` and only `success` calibrates a budget; the checker rejects any other value by name rather than skipping it. A failed or cancelled run stopped early, so its duration bounds the work from below instead of measuring it -- recording a two-minute setup failure would otherwise justify a four-minute ceiling for a job that needs thirty-five. Every run recorded below completed successfully.",
  "not_claimed": "This is a sample, not a distribution with a tail. The CI job's 27 observations span 12 hours on one runner pool; the two release-workflow jobs have n=1 each, because v0.8.0 is the only release run to complete since the budget moved. The half-rule margin is what covers the difference between a sample maximum and a real worst case, and it is a margin rather than a percentile because nobody has measured a percentile here.",
